2026-09-28: bounded enabled-connector diagnosis, not a rewritten upstream PASS.

The original proc_filter.c is a persistent listener: main loops until SIGINT,
then unsubscribes, prints its count and returns zero. With the new config it
receives process events but still times out under the unchanged original
45-second runner limit. That original FAIL/TIMEOUT remains in its receipt.

The small C fixture performs two distinct controls:
  1. subscribe using the same process connector API; require a successful ACK,
     then match the fork and exit (status 23) of an owned child; unsubscribe.
  2. execute the unchanged original binary/argv; create 20 bounded owned child
     events, prove the listener has not spontaneously exited, send SIGINT only
     to that owned listener, and require normal completion within five seconds
     plus matching child PID/parent/exit records and an untruncated final count.
The second is an explicitly controlled diagnostic, not the original runner.

Sealed GCC14 and Clang19 -std=gnu11 -O2 -static -Wall -Wextra -Werror builds
pass. Both binaries execute on the new reference Image: 10/10 checks each,
20/20 total, with normal shutdown. The listener sees all 20 owned children in
each run. No kernel/test source, original program, timeout or denominator is
changed. Only GCC reference acceptance is established here; new EvoCC replay
still awaits final composition.

The first compile catches a Clang signed-comparison warning in NLMSG_OK; the
explicitly nonnegative receive length is now cast for that macro. The initial
runtime control also wrongly assumes response seq echoes the request. Frozen
cn_proc.c:send_msg overwrites seq with a per-CPU counter. The corrected probe
checks the matching request ack + 1, which cn_proc_ack actually preserves.
Both initial controls fail 3/10 assertions; their source/log/receipt remain.
These are fixture repairs, not claimed compiler/kernel defects.

